ollowed that  rule for many years but then when I was  writing Ice and Fire I said wait a  minute I'm planning on having more than  26 characters  Wow what am I gonna do here am I gonna  named him like X Fred or so I decided to  ignore that rule in fact I decided  reading history I decided to go even  further if it's bad to name two  characters characters names that start  with the same letter it's really bad to  give two characters the same story the  same name so I looked at you know  reading English history which was  inspiration for that if you read English  history and you guys are Canadians I  presume you know a little of English  history everybody ended is named Edward  and Henry it's really you know the  history of England is a history of  Edward and Henry and occasionally  richard sneaks in and does a little  mischief and somehow yet we keep them we  attach numbers to them we attach you  know secondary names you know Edward of  Woodstock Henry of York or whatever and  we're able to distinguish one from the  other or with nicknames and I said you  know I'm gonna do that that's much more  realistic I'm gonna have these these  family names and and like many families  I know in the real world there'll be I'm  naming UF your grandmother I'm naming  you after your grandfather there was  this great king and you want to curry  favor so of course you're you're gonna  name your child after the king and I  wanted to show all these naming patterns  and they all occur in in the book why  will you see you know Robert comes to  the throne and a lot of children around  the same time suddenly seem to have the  name Robert in numerous families but you  also see families like the Starks who  keep having a brand in every every  generation every other generation  there's a Brandon of some sort going  back to their ancestor bran the Builder  and the Lannisters who always have to  tea why you know Tywin him all to Tyrion  so forth so the echoes of family names  and family naming 